Here is the easiest possible way to do this. You only need to update 2 tiny
spots in your file (plus 1 optional spot if you also want typed box text and
tabs to carry over into the exported copy).

The trick is simple: your file already has a #seedData block that gets rewritten
every time you click Download Standalone App (.html). If we store a freshly
generated key inside #seedData during export, every exported HTML file
automatically gets its own private localStorage slot.

Step 1: Tell the app to use the file's own unique key

Find this line (in your <script> section, right below const seed = ...):

const STORAGE_KEY = 'digitalChat_autoClaims_v2';

Replace it with:

const STORAGE_KEY = seed.storageKey || 'digitalChat_autoClaims_v2';

What this does:

  - Your original file doesn't have a storageKey in #seedData yet, so it keeps
    using 'digitalChat_autoClaims_v2' (no existing data lost).
  - Any exported file will have its own seed.storageKey, so it uses that
    instead.

Step 2: Generate a brand-new key every time you export

Find this line inside function downloadUpdatedHtml(btn):

clone.querySelector('#seedData').textContent = JSON.stringify({ boxHeadings, defaultCardColors: cardColors, boxSequences }, null, 4);

Replace it with these 2 lines:

const newKey = 'digitalChat_autoClaims_' + Date.now() + '_' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 6);
clone.querySelector('#seedData').textContent = JSON.stringify({ storageKey: newKey, boxHeadings, defaultCardColors: cardColors, boxSequences }, null, 4);

What this does:

  - Every time you export, Date.now() + a random 4-character code creates a
    unique ID (for example: digitalChat_autoClaims_1744000123456_a9f2).
  - That key is baked only into the downloaded HTML file, leaving your current
    file untouched.
  - Now, clicking Reset Defaults inside File 2 only deletes File 2's key—File 1
    stays completely safe.

Step 3 (Optional, Recommended): Carry over your typed box text into the new file

Because the exported file now has a brand-new empty storage key, opening it for
the first time will load your dropdown options/headings, not unsaved text you
typed directly into the boxes or extra tabs—unless you add this quick step too.

A. In downloadUpdatedHtml(btn) (the same line from Step 2), add savedState:
state, DEFAULT_VARS:

const newKey = 'digitalChat_autoClaims_' + Date.now() + '_' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 6);
clone.querySelector('#seedData').textContent = JSON.stringify({ storageKey: newKey, boxHeadings, defaultCardColors: cardColors, boxSequences, savedState: state, DEFAULT_VARS }, null, 4);

B. Inside function restoreBrowserData(), find these lines:

const saved = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
if (!saved) {
    setupDefaultSheets();
    applyAllCardColors();
    return;
}
const data = JSON.parse(saved);

Replace them with:

const saved = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
if (!saved && !seed.savedState) {
    setupDefaultSheets();
    applyAllCardColors();
    return;
}
const data = saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : { state: seed.savedState, DEFAULT_VARS: seed.DEFAULT_VARS };

What this does:

  - The very first time you open the downloaded file, localStorage is empty for
    that new key, so it loads the exact boxes, tabs, and variables you exported
    (seed.savedState).
  - As soon as you type anything, it saves to that file's own separate
    localStorage key.
  - Even if you wipe your browser's site data later, reopening the downloaded
    HTML restores it right back to the snapshot baked inside that file.

Quick Reminder on Testing

1.  Save these changes to your main HTML file and refresh it in the browser.
2.  Export Copy 1 and Copy 2.
3.  Open both—any edits or Reset Defaults clicks in one copy will no longer
    affect the other. (Note: If you use your browser settings to "Clear all
    browsing data" for local files, the browser still wipes localStorage for all
    file:// pages at once—which is why Step 3 is so helpful, because each file
    keeps its exported snapshot inside the HTML itself.)
